# answered by claude-opus-5-5, prompt /lib/phil/senses b350a3107fa4, 2026-10-19
under_discipline	one
ground_in_law	one
ground_elsewhere	one
only_duty	one
subjects	one
# ground_in_law: S2 states it as a requirement ("must not ... set the determining principle ... anywhere else"). P4, P5 and A1 all read it the same normative way, so it stays in one sense.
# subjects: "subjects" means bound under the law's authority rather than sovereign over it. P6, P7 and A1 all use it that way, even though P6 also calls us legislative members.
